<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  NOT sandboxed, and that is a decision rather than an omission.

	  Revis shipped sandboxed for its first releases, with no network entitlement
	  at all, and both facts were asserted by scripts/release.sh. What bought them
	  back was the in-app updater: a sandboxed app cannot replace itself in
	  /Applications — `isWritableFile` answers false from inside a container and
	  the write fails with NSFileWriteNoPermissionError — so an in-place update can
	  only ever be refused at its very last step. Vaelora next door hit exactly
	  this and dropped its sandbox for the same reason; see AppUpdater.

	  So the containment that mattered stayed and moved DOWN, to where the
	  untrusted thing actually is. The document is still made inert by the
	  sanitizer before a byte reaches WebKit, still served under `default-src
	  'none'` with `script-src 'none'` and no `connect-src`, still rendered in a
	  non-persistent data store by a navigation delegate that cancels every load
	  after the first, and the review runtime still lives in its own
	  WKContentWorld. A page that cannot fetch, navigate, or run script does not
	  reach this entitlement whatever the app is allowed to do. What the sandbox
	  was covering here was the app's own bugs, not the page's — and it was
	  covering them at the price of never being able to ship a fix automatically.
	-->

	<!--
	  Outbound only, and used in exactly two places: reading the update manifest
	  and downloading the archive named in it (UpdateChecker, AppUpdater). There is
	  no server side to this app.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
